======================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================

FIT: the fused device arm wins at every rung, 1.04x-5.78x, and the margin grows
with n_classes exactly as the shared-Gram design predicts — at d=64 the host
arm goes 81 -> 267 ms from k=3 to k=26 (its O(n*d*K) XtY term) while the device
arm goes 36.8 -> 55.3. Forming the Gram ONCE is what that difference measures.

PREDICT: wins in a k>=5, d<=128 rectangle (1.20x-6.31x, six rungs, no loss),
loses outside it. That is the shipped dispatch gate. This is the first predict
in the codebase where a device arm beats the host matvec at all — single-target
Ridge predict measured 10-23x SLOWER on a P100 — and the two things that
changed are both linear in K: K times the compute over the same transfer, and
a fused argmax that shrinks the egress from m*k floats to m i32 labels.

CAVEAT, and it is not small: the host column is the same code the cpu backend
runs, but on Colab's 2-vCPU Xeon @2GHz. RIDGE-DEFAULT-CUDA measured the
16-thread dev box at roughly 4x that on the same host arm. Divided by 4, the
device fit would win ~1.2-1.4x at k=26 and LOSE at k<=3. That division is an
extrapolation, not a measurement, and the local ladder

    cargo test -p mlrs-algos --release --features cpu \
      --test ridge_classifier_cuda_perf_test -- --ignored --nocapture

is what would replace it with one. It was not run for this log: the dev box was
saturated by a co-tenant job (load average 250-370 throughout), and a loaded
box has inverted an mlrs-vs-sklearn verdict in this project before.
